-- title:
--   Uniform bound on the size of a town ball
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$, a town scale $h$, a block size $\ell$, a centre $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ and a radius $R \in \mathbb{R}$. Write $\|x\|_\infty = \max_i |x_i|$, let
--   $$Q_\ell(x) \;=\; \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \{\, y : \|y-x\|_\infty \le \ell/2 \,\} \qquad (\texttt{block } \ell\ x)$$
--   be the block of size $\ell$ based at $x$, and let $T_{h,\ell}(x) = Q_\ell(h\,x)$ (`townIndex h ℓ x`) be the block of the town indexed by $x$. The **town ball** is the collection of blocks whose index lies within $R$ of $z$,
--   $$\mathrm{TownBall}(h,\ell,z,R) \;=\; \bigl\{\, Q_\ell(h\,p) \;:\; p \in \mathbb{Z}^d,\ \|p - z\| < R \,\bigr\} \qquad (\texttt{townBall } h\ \ell\ z\ R),$$
--   a set of *subsets* of $\mathbb{R}^d$ (the index $p$, not the block itself, is what is required to be within $R$ of $z$). Then
--   $$\#\,\mathrm{TownBall}(h,\ell,z,R) \;\le\; \bigl(2\lceil R\rceil + 1\bigr)^{d},$$
--   with $\lceil\cdot\rceil$ the least natural number at least the argument (so $\lceil R\rceil = 0$ for $R \le 0$).
--
--   The bound is the lattice counting bound transported to the renormalized level — it counts the indices $p \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ with $\|p-z\| < R$, of which there are at most $2\lceil R\rceil + 1$ in each of the $d$ coordinates — and it has the same two features that make it usable: it is independent of the centre $z$, and it is independent of the two size parameters $h$ and $\ell$, so the same constant serves at every scale of the renormalization. Bounds like this are what give the length bounds in path-construction arguments, where a walk confined to a finite vertex set can be shortened to a path whose length is bounded by the size of that set.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypotheses at all are imposed: $h$, $\ell$ and $R$ are arbitrary reals, including negative or zero values, for which the collection degenerates. The cardinality is `Set.ncard`, which is $0$ on an infinite set; the content of the statement is therefore that the collection is finite with the stated bound whenever it is finite by the construction. Since the town ball is the *image* of a set of indices, distinct indices giving the same block are counted once, so the bound need not be attained.
-- source:
